WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:30.430
<v Ira Glass>From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life, distributed by Public Radio International. I'm Ira Glass. Here is a recording you weren't meant to hear. A guy from New York, a percussionist, went to perform in this opera in Italy. While he was there he met this woman, who was a graduate student, who was writing about the opera for her thesis. She took him on a tour of Rome on the back of her scooter. He met her friends. They hung out. One thing led to another. They had this week together. And when he got home to New York, he didn't want it to be over. So he made her these tapes.
<v Ira Glass>来自芝加哥WBEZ电台，《美国生活》节目，由国际公共广播电台发行。我是艾拉·格拉斯。下面这段录音原本不该被你听到。一位来自纽约的打击乐手，去意大利参加一场歌剧演出。他在那儿遇到了一位女研究生，她当时正在为自己的论文撰写关于这场歌剧的内容。她骑着小踏板车带他游览了罗马，他见到了她的朋友们，他们在一起玩，事情就这样自然而然地发生了。他们在一起度过了一周。当他回到纽约的家中时，他不想这段感情就此结束。于是，他为她录制了这些磁带。

00:00:30.430 --> 00:00:47.350
<v David  Cossin>It's scary. It's scary how much I'm thinking of you. But it's good. It makes me happy. Even if you're not here, I feel happy to think about you and to know that you're existing somewhere.
<v David  Cossin>这很吓人。想到你这么多，这真的很吓人。但这种感觉很好。这让我感到快乐。即使你不在身边，想到你，知道你在某个地方存在着，我就觉得很幸福。

00:00:47.350 --> 00:01:17.930
<v Ira Glass>When you're really love and other people overhear you, I think it's hard not to sound a little over the top. That's what I like about these recordings. The feeling in them is so direct. And so today we're going to try something completely different on our radio show. Every story on the show is made out of tapes like this one. Every story is made from tapes that were intended for an audience of one. Someone somewhere made somebody else a tape. We listen in, all of us. I have to say, it's kind of an amazing show because of that.
<v Ira Glass>当你真正坠入爱河而又被他人偷听到时，我觉得很难不显得有些夸张。这就是我喜欢这些录音的原因。其中的情感非常直接。所以今天，我们要在这个广播节目中尝试一些完全不同的东西。节目中的每个故事都由像这样的一盘盘磁带组成。每个故事都源自那些原本只打算给某一个人听的录音。某人在某处为某人录下了一盘磁带。而我们，我们所有人，都在一旁旁听。我必须得说，正因为如此，这真是一档了不起的节目。

00:01:17.930 --> 00:02:00.270
<v Ira Glass>Act One, we hear the greatest phone message in the world, the absolute greatest. Act Two, we have a special effects story. In that act, we use the power of audio technology to turn someone who stutters into someone who no longer stutters, so he can deliver a message to one man in Idaho. Act Three, a war story. In fact, the story of a soldier in the first Persian Gulf War who made tapes for his wife during battles, until he accidentally recorded this incident, this violent incident that haunted him for years. And Act Four, a love story. In that act we return to the guy who you just heard from a minute ago, a man trying to sustain a relationship that spans oceans, all with a little cassette tape.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，我们将听到世界上最棒的电话留言，绝对是最棒的。第二幕，我们有一个关于特效的故事。在那一幕中，我们将利用音频技术的力量，让一个口吃的人不再口吃，这样他就可以给爱达荷州的一个人传达信息。第三幕，一个战争故事。事实上，这是一个士兵在第一次海湾战争期间的故事，他在战斗期间为妻子录制磁带，直到他偶然录下了这一事件，这一困扰了他多年的暴力事件。第四幕，一个爱情故事。在那一幕中，我们将回到你刚才听到的那个家伙身边，一个试图通过一盘小小的磁带，维系一段跨洋恋情的男人。

00:02:00.270 --> 00:02:24.830
<v Ira Glass>And so without further ado, let us turn now to Act One. This Act One is the story of the greatest phone message of all time. Some people see it that way anyway. You may judge for yourself. A quick warning that there is one famously nasty word in this story that occurs exactly seven times. Count them yourself. But do not worry. We beep the word every single time. Producer Jonathan Goldstein tells the tale.
<v Ira Glass>那么，事不宜迟，让我们转向第一幕。这一幕讲述的是史上最棒的电话留言的故事。至少有些人是这么认为的。你可以自行评判。在此预先警告，这个故事里有一个著名的肮脏词汇，出现了整整七次。你可以自己数数。但别担心，我们每次都会把它消音。制作人乔纳森·戈尔德斯坦将为你讲述这个故事。

00:02:24.830 --> 00:02:59.770
<v Jonathan Goldstein>The first thing you should know about my friend Josh is that he calls me a bitch squealer. Now bitch isn't the bad word you're going to be hearing in this story. And that's because it's referring to an actual dog, a fenced in security dog that barked at me and Josh while we were out walking one night. And while my scream may have been louder that evening, Josh's scream was definitely higher pitched, which to my mind means Josh should rightfully be called the bitch squealer, while perhaps I should be called something like bitch bellower or bitch loud crier. Just the same, quit your bitch squealing is what Josh says to me when I ask him to please change the station on the car radio or to stop crowding the armrest in the movie theater.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>关于我的朋友乔什，首先你要知道的是，他管我叫“婊子尖叫者”（bitch squealer）。别误会，这里的“婊子”可不是你在这个故事里即将听到的那个坏词。那是因为它指的是一只真正的狗，一只被关在围栏里的看门狗，有一天晚上我和乔什在外面散步时它对我们狂吠。虽然那天晚上我的尖叫声可能更大，但乔什的尖叫声绝对音调更高，在我看来，这意味着乔什理应被称为“婊子尖叫者”，而我或许应该被称为“婊子咆哮者”或者“婊子大叫者”。即便如此，当我要求他换一下车上的广播电台，或者在电影院里别挤着扶手时，乔什还是会冲我说：“别再像婊子一样尖叫了。”

00:02:59.770 --> 00:03:30.700
<v Jonathan Goldstein>The other thing to know about Josh is that he thinks of himself as an idea man. And he always refers to his ideas as pure gold. So a few years ago, when I first started doing stories on the radio, I would call him up and ask him if he had any story ideas. And he always did. The thing was, most of them involved hot dog eating contests and all-nude car washes. One time Josh talked to a French-Canadian waitress who used the words diggy do as a conjunctive phrase, as in, my mother, she gave birth to me in Lac Saint-Louis, and diggy do, I'm in Montreal. Josh tried to convince me that this semantically innovative young woman was most definitely worthy of a 40 minute interview on national radio.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>关于乔什，还要知道的另一件事是，他自认为是个有想法的人。他总是把自己的点子称为“纯金”。所以几年前，当我刚开始做广播故事时，我会打电话问他有没有什么故事点子。他总是有。问题是，其中大多数都涉及吃热狗比赛和全裸洗车。有一次，乔什和一个法裔加拿大女服务员聊过天，她用“diggy do”作为连词，比如，“我母亲在圣路易斯湖生下了我，diggy do，我就在蒙特利尔了。”乔什试图说服我，这位语义创新的年轻女性绝对值得在国家电台接受四十分钟的采访。

00:03:30.700 --> 00:03:43.820
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Just the other day, Josh was telling me about this really funny phone message that he heard back in his college days, and how I should definitely do a radio show about that. He swore to me that it was the defining moment in his class' campus life that year. Now how is a person supposed to believe something like that?
<v Jonathan Goldstein>就在前几天，乔什还跟我提起他在大学时代听到的那个非常有趣的电话留言，说我一定要为此做一期广播节目。他向我发誓，那是他那一届同学校园生活中最具决定性的时刻。可是一个人怎么可能相信这种事呢？

00:03:43.820 --> 00:03:46.080
<v Jonathan Goldstein>I can imagine you really liking this message.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>我可以想象你会非常喜欢这个留言。

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:47.960
<v Josh>Oh, I see. I see.
<v Josh>哦，我明白了。我明白了。

00:03:47.960 --> 00:03:52.460
<v Jonathan  Goldstein>But you see, I can't imagine it being the kind of thing that was like--
<v Jonathan  Goldstein>但是你看，我无法想象它会是那种……

00:03:52.460 --> 00:03:55.430
<v Josh>That your sedate NPR audience would appreciate?
<v Josh>那种你们那沉稳的NPR听众会欣赏的东西？

00:03:55.430 --> 00:04:00.620
<v Jonathan Goldstein>No, no, no. I mean it sounds like you got a kick out of it at the time. But I can't imagine it being like an atomic bomb that hit the campus or something.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>不，不，不。我是说听起来你当时确实觉得很有趣。但我无法想象它会像一颗原子弹落在校园里那样震撼。

00:04:00.620 --> 00:04:07.070
<v Josh>Yeah. See, this is clearly another example of the failure of your imagination. How many times have I given you ideas that you have nay said? How many times have I given you gold standard ideas--
<v Josh>是的。看，这显然又是一个你想象力匮乏的例子。我给你提过多少次你拒绝的建议了？我给你提供过多少次纯金标准的点子——

00:04:07.070 --> 00:04:32.010
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Josh yells at me a lot, especially when he thinks I'm not taking his ideas seriously. When we go out to eat, he yells at me loud enough to make the other patrons turn around and look at us. Sometimes though, he'll get all unexpectedly silent, and just stare out the restaurant window, and then turn to me and say something like, wouldn't life be better if there was a big old pig sitting out there by the fire hydrant? Why can't life be more like that?
<v Jonathan Goldstein>乔什经常对我大吼大叫，尤其是在他觉得我没有认真对待他的点子时。当我们出去吃饭时，他吼得足够大声，让其他顾客都回头看我们。不过有时，他会突然变得异常安静，盯着餐厅窗外看，然后转头对我说一些类似这样的话：“如果窗外消防栓旁坐着一头大肥猪，生活会不会更好呢？为什么生活不能更像那样呢？”

00:04:32.010 --> 00:04:44.300
<v Jonathan Goldstein>But anyway, to get back to the phone message, the one Josh heard in college, I'm telling you about it not to demonstrate what a slightly misguided colorful character Josh is, but to chart with honesty the unfairness of my pre-emptive bitch squeals of doubt.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>但不管怎样，回到那个电话留言，就是乔什大学时代听到的那个。我提起它并不是为了展示乔什是一个有点古怪且富有色彩的人物，而是为了坦诚地记录我那些先入为主的、充满怀疑的“婊子尖叫”。

00:04:44.300 --> 00:05:04.960
<v Josh>I went to Columbia University in the early '90s, OK? Late '80s, early '90s. When I was there, they had this phone system-- I'll just give you a little bit of background, all right? And then I'll cut to the chase. They has this system there called the Rolm system, Rolm phone system, R-O-L-M. And you could forward messages to people. You could forward messages to everyone on campus if you wanted.
<v Josh>我在90年代初上的哥伦比亚大学，好吗？80年代末，90年代初。我在那里的时候，他们有这样一个电话系统——我先给你简单介绍点背景，好吗？然后我就切入正题。他们那里有一个叫Rolm系统的东西，Rolm电话系统，R-O-L-M。你可以把留言转发给别人。如果你愿意，你可以转发给校园里的每个人。

00:05:04.960 --> 00:05:08.140
<v Jonathan  Goldstein>Sort of like a precursor to the internet.
<v Jonathan  Goldstein>有点像互联网的前身。

00:05:08.140 --> 00:05:42.420
<v Josh>Yes, like a precursor to the internet. Thank you, Mr. Current Affairs Guy. So it was an amazing utility. People could forward all kinds of crazy messages. So one day, there was this guy named Fred. And his mother left him a message on his answering machine. And he forwarded it to maybe one or more of his friends. And his friends turned around and did a Brutus, and they stabbed him in the back. And they forwarded this message across campus to everyone.
<v Josh>是的，就像互联网的前身。谢谢你，时事先生。所以这是一个非常神奇的工具。人们可以转发各种疯狂的留言。有一天，有个叫弗雷德的家伙。他妈妈在他的答录机上留了一条信息。他把它转发给了他的一位或几位朋友。然后他的朋友们反过来对他做了个“布鲁图斯”，在背后捅了他一刀。他们把这条信息转发给了全校每一个人。

00:05:42.420 --> 00:05:45.470
<v Josh>So do you want to hear the message? All right. So he prefaced it by saying--
<v Josh>那你想听听那条留言吗？好吧。他之前加了一句前言，他说——

00:05:45.470 --> 00:05:47.660
<v Jonathan Goldstein>You have it? You have the message?
<v Jonathan Goldstein>你有录音？你有那条留言？

00:05:47.660 --> 00:05:53.530
<v Josh>I do not have the message. I have the message in my head. I am telling you a story. All right?
<v Josh>我没有留言。留言在我脑子里。我在给你讲故事。好吗？

00:05:53.530 --> 00:05:54.930
<v Jonathan Goldstein>OK.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>好的。

00:05:54.930 --> 00:06:10.310
<v Josh>All right. So he prefaced it by some kind of a sad little lead in. In a little voice, he was like, I think you'd would appreciate hearing this message from my mother. And then the message played. This was the entirety of the message. And I'm going to do the voice for you as best I can. You ready?
<v Josh>好了。所以他在前面加了一段悲伤的小导语。他用一种小小的声音说：“我想你们会喜欢听我母亲留的这条信息。”然后信息就开始播放了。这就是信息的全部内容。我会尽我所能模仿那个声音。准备好了吗？

00:06:10.310 --> 00:06:11.430
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Yeah.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>好了。

00:06:11.430 --> 00:06:37.840
<v Josh>Oh, sorry. More background. Apparently he was not a hit with the ladies, Fred. This is what I was led to understand. I'm not sure if this is true or not. But he had managed to score a date to go see The Little Mermaid, of all movies. The Little Mermaid. OK? So this is the message his own mother, his blood relation, leaves for him.
<v Josh>哦，抱歉。再多加点背景。显然他在女生中并不受欢迎，弗雷德。我是这么理解的。我不确定这是不是真的。但他好不容易约到了一个女生去电影院看《小美人鱼》，偏偏是这部电影。《小美人鱼》。好吗？这就是他亲生母亲，他的血亲，留给他的留言。

00:06:37.840 --> 00:06:50.970
<v Josh>And I quote, "You and The Little Mermaid can both go [BLEEP] yourselves. The books you wanted, they're not here. They must be in La Jolla. I'm not going to wait up all night for you. Goodbye." That's the entirety of it. All right?
<v Josh>我引用一下：“你和《小美人鱼》都去[消音]吧。你想要的那些书，不在这儿。肯定在拉霍亚（La Jolla）。我不会整晚在这儿等你。再见。”这就是全部内容。好吗？

00:06:50.970 --> 00:06:53.320
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Yeah. That's the message that his mother left him?
<v Jonathan Goldstein>是的。这就是他妈妈留给他的留言？

00:06:53.320 --> 00:07:08.470
<v Josh>That's correct. You catch that part? "You and The Little Mermaid can both go [BLEEP] yourselves." I love you, son. That's gold.
<v Josh>没错。你抓住那部分了吗？“你和《小美人鱼》都去[消音]吧。”我爱你，儿子。这真是金子。

00:07:08.470 --> 00:07:11.250
<v Josh>And then-- no, hold on. Are you going to listen?
<v Josh>然后——不，等等。你在听吗？

00:07:11.250 --> 00:07:12.920
<v Jonathan  Goldstein>Yes.
<v Jonathan  Goldstein>在听。

00:07:12.920 --> 00:07:31.500
<v Josh>Then somebody took it-- some evil mix-meister genius took it and remixed it into a 12 inch dance version. "You and The Little Mermaid, La Jolla, La Jolla, [BLEEP] yourselves, [BLEEP] yourselves. They're not here, the books. Goodbye, goodbye, goodbye, goodbye.
<v Josh>然后有人把它——某个邪恶的混音天才把它拿去混音成了一个12英寸的舞曲版本。“你和《小美人鱼》，拉霍亚，拉霍亚，[消音]你们自己，[消音]你们自己。它们不在这儿，那些书。再见，再见，再见，再见。”

00:07:31.500 --> 00:07:38.860
<v Jonathan Goldstein>And there are other people who remember it?
<v Jonathan Goldstein>还有其他人记得这件事吗？

00:07:38.860 --> 00:07:58.550
<v Josh>Are you even listening to a word I just told you? This was the The Producers of its day, OK? Everyone heard about it. Everyone knew it. Everyone had an opinion about it. Every single person who attended Columbia that year, I guarantee you they would know what I'm talking about.
<v Josh>你到底有没有在听我说话？这可是当年的《制片人》（The Producers），懂吗？每个人都听说过。每个人都知道。每个人对此都有看法。我保证，那一年在哥伦比亚大学上学的每一个人，都知道我在说什么。

00:07:58.550 --> 00:08:22.870
<v Jonathan   Goldstein>I still didn't believe him. But just for the hell of it, I phoned the Columbia alumni magazine to see if there was anyone there who might remember anything about the Little Mermaid message. I ended up speaking to someone who not only attended Columbia in the early '90s and remembered the message, but just like Josh had, he actually quoted it to me, the whole message.
<v Jonathan   Goldstein>我还是不相信他。但出于好奇，我打电话给哥伦比亚大学的校友杂志，想看看有没有人记得关于《小美人鱼》留言的事。结果我和一个人聊上了，他不仅在90年代初上过哥伦比亚大学并记得这件事，而且就像乔什一样，他竟然把整段留言一字不差地背给了我听。

00:08:22.870 --> 00:08:50.340
<v Jonathan   Goldstein>"You and The Little Mermaid can both go [BLEEP] yourselves. I can't find the books. They must be in La Jolla. I'm not going to wait up all night for you. Goodbye." The guy then became so excited at the thought of someone doing serious research about the message that he offered to use the Columbia database to look up every Fred that might have graduated around that time. No matter how long it took, he said, it would be worth it if I could track down some recording of the message and allow him to hear it again.
<v Jonathan   Goldstein>“你和《小美人鱼》都去[消音]吧。我找不到那些书。它们肯定在拉霍亚。我不会整晚在这儿等你。再见。”那家伙后来对有人如此认真地研究这个留言感到非常兴奋，他主动提出利用哥伦比亚大学的数据库，去查找大约在那一年毕业的所有叫弗雷德的人。他说，无论花多长时间，只要能找到这条留言的录音，让他能再听一次，一切都值得。

00:08:50.340 --> 00:08:56.470
<v Jonathan   Goldstein>[PHONE DIALING]
<v Jonathan   Goldstein>[拨号声]

00:08:56.470 --> 00:09:04.240
<v Jonathan   Goldstein>I called other Columbia students from that period. And every one of them reacted the same way to the message, like this guy Ben Feldman, now an entertainment lawyer.
<v Jonathan   Goldstein>我又给当时的其他哥伦比亚大学学生打了电话。他们每一个人的反应都和那个人一样，比如现在担任娱乐律师的本·费尔德曼。

00:09:04.240 --> 00:09:05.020
<v Ben Feldman>Hello.
<v Ben Feldman>喂。

00:09:05.020 --> 00:09:07.150
<v Jonathan  Goldstein>Is this Ben?
<v Jonathan  Goldstein>是本吗？

00:09:07.150 --> 00:09:07.810
<v Ben Feldman>Yes.
<v Ben Feldman>对。

00:09:07.810 --> 00:09:14.310
<v Jonathan Goldstein>I have two words for you, little mermaid.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>我有两个词送给你：小美人鱼。

00:09:14.310 --> 00:09:23.950
<v Ben Feldman>This is the funniest call I've ever received. Well, you and The Little Mermaid can go to hell.
<v Ben Feldman>这是我接过的最搞笑的电话。好吧，你和《小美人鱼》都见鬼去吧。

00:09:23.950 --> 00:09:48.860
<v Jonathan  Goldstein>A few days later, Josh called me back. He had found out Fred's last name from his older brother, who it turns out graduated the same year as Fred. Josh said that Fred's last name was Schultz. And I told Josh that this was great news. And Josh told me to shut my squeal hole, which I did.
<v Jonathan  Goldstein>几天后，乔什又给我打了电话。他从他哥哥那里打听到了弗雷德的姓氏，他哥哥碰巧和弗雷德同一年毕业。乔什说弗雷德姓舒尔茨。我告诉乔什这是个好消息。然后乔什叫我闭上那张尖叫的嘴，我也照做了。

00:09:48.860 --> 00:10:08.050
<v Jonathan  Goldstein>So I called Fred Schultz. And it turns out that he had recorded the original message and still had a copy of it, a copy which I am now going to play for you. Remember, this is a phone message that was forwarded from one person to the next, each person re-prefacing the previous prefaces as it made its way from one voice mail message box to the next.
<v Jonathan  Goldstein>于是我给弗雷德·舒尔茨打了电话。原来他录下了原始留言，并且还保留着一份拷贝，我现在就为你播放这一份。请记住，这是一条在人与人之间转发的电话留言，每个人在转发时都会在前面加上自己的前言，就这样它从一个语音信箱传到了另一个语音信箱。

00:10:08.050 --> 00:10:10.050
<v Voice Mail Box>Recieved at 4:20 PM Friday.
<v Voice Mail Box>星期五下午4:20收到。

00:10:10.050 --> 00:10:17.940
<v Woman 1>Guys, I have never heard a phone mail message like this one. Listen to the first person. You are going to die.
<v Woman 1>伙计们，我从来没听过这样的电话留言。听听第一个人说的。你会笑死的。

00:10:17.940 --> 00:10:20.790
<v Man 1>No seriously, this is the funniest one of all of them.
<v Man 1>不，说真的，这是所有留言里最搞笑的一个。

00:10:20.790 --> 00:10:21.860
<v Woman 2>All right, here it is--
<v Woman 2>好了，就是这个——

00:10:21.860 --> 00:10:39.510
<v Jonathan Goldstein>These giddy introductory messages continue for two and 1/2 minutes, each one revving up the impending drama, acting as a kind of stage curtain that opens onto another curtain, and yet another one still, each one teasing you with the tantalizing proximity of the main stage about to be bathed in the spotlight.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>这些令人兴奋的开场白持续了两分半钟，每一段都在推高即将到来的戏剧性，就像一幕幕开启的舞台幕布，一层套着一层，每一层都在撩拨着你的神经，暗示着主角即将登场。

00:10:39.510 --> 00:10:45.110
<v Man 2>OK, I've gotten like 95 phone mail messages in the last two days, but this is the funniest.
<v Man 2>好了，我这两天收到了大概95条语音留言，但这一个最搞笑。

00:10:45.110 --> 00:10:49.320
<v Man 3>This is going to blow you away. This makes the other ones look like chopped liver.
<v Man 3>这绝对会让你惊掉下巴。其他的那些在它面前简直不值一提。

00:10:49.320 --> 00:10:59.430
<v Jonathan Goldstein>And then finally the chain of deferral ends with the very first forwarded student's solemn pronouncement.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>最后，这一连串的转发在第一个转发的学生那严肃的宣告声中结束。

00:10:59.430 --> 00:11:15.730
<v Man 4>There comes a time in life when we hear the greatest phone mail message of all time. And well, here it is. You have to hear it to believe it.
<v Man 4>人的一生中总会遇到一次，听到史上最棒的电话留言。嗯，就是这个。你得亲耳听听才能相信。

00:11:15.730 --> 00:11:22.410
<v Fred  Schultz>I thought you'd get a kick out of this message from my mother.
<v Fred  Schultz>我想你会喜欢我母亲留的这条信息的。

00:11:22.410 --> 00:11:38.830
<v Joan Schultz>Hi, Fred. You and The Little Mermaid can go [BLEEP] yourselves. I told you to stay near the phone. I can't find those books. You have other books here. It must be in La Jolla. Call me back. I'm not going to stay up all night for you. Goodbye.
<v Joan Schultz>你好，弗雷德。你和《小美人鱼》都去[消音]吧。我告诉过你待在电话旁边。我找不到那些书。你这儿还有别的书。肯定是在拉霍亚。给我回电话。我不会整晚不睡等着你。再见。

00:11:38.830 --> 00:12:03.830
<v Jonathan Goldstein>These days, Fred Schultz lives in Venice Beach, California. He plays in a band, he skateboards, and he pretty much seems happy. When he sent me the recording of the Little Mermaid message, he also included burnt incense and a CD of his band's soundtrack for a film about cannibalism called Eat Me. Here's a clip. [MUSIC - "EAT ME"]
<v Jonathan Goldstein>如今，弗雷德·舒尔茨住在加利福尼亚州的威尼斯海滩。他组了个乐队，玩滑板，看起来过得很幸福。当他把《小美人鱼》留言的录音寄给我时，还附带了熏香和他乐队为一部关于食人的电影《吃了我》（Eat Me）制作的原声带CD。这是一段片段。[音乐 - "EAT ME"]

00:12:03.830 --> 00:12:20.670
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Fred is the kind of guy who, when the subject gets on to future plans, will tell you he's thinking pretty seriously about moving onto a boat. When I ask him about the phone message from his mom, he says that from the moment he got it, he knew he was sitting on something big. The question then became, what was he going to do with it?
<v Jonathan Goldstein>弗雷德属于那种当话题谈到未来计划时，会告诉你他正在认真考虑搬到船上住的人。当我问他关于他妈妈那条电话留言的事时，他说从收到留言的那一刻起，他就知道自己手里握着个大新闻。接下来的问题是，他该拿它怎么办？

00:12:20.670 --> 00:12:40.380
<v Fred Schultz>I did sit down and stress and think about it for like an hour or two. I debated whether to send the message out to anyone. And then I sat down and listened to music, and just thought about it, re-listening to the message and just thinking, should I send this along or just let it die, kill it, hit erase.
<v Fred Schultz>我确实坐下来苦思冥想了一两个小时。我纠结着要不要把它发给别人。然后我坐下来听音乐，思考着这件事，反复听着那条留言，心想：我是该把它传下去，还是让它死掉、杀掉它、直接按删除键。

00:12:40.380 --> 00:13:02.810
<v Jonathan Goldstein>But Fred decided not to hit erase. And he explains his mother's cryptic message this way. He had called looking for an old school notebook. She said she'd search for it, but only if he'd wait by the phone while she did this special favor for him. But did he stay by the phone? No, he did not. As for The Little Mermaid, this was the one thing Josh was completely wrong about.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>但弗雷德决定不按删除键。他这样解释他母亲那条隐晦的留言：他当时打电话想找一本旧学校笔记。她说她会去找，但条件是他得待在电话旁等她做这个特殊的忙。但他守在电话旁了吗？没有，他没有。至于《小美人鱼》，这是乔什完全搞错的一点。

00:13:02.810 --> 00:13:07.190
<v Fred Schultz>My whole life, I have had a thing about mermaids-- and dolphins, but mermaids.
<v Fred Schultz>我这一生，对美人鱼一直有种情结——还有海豚，但主要是美人鱼。

00:13:07.190 --> 00:13:13.060
<v Jonathan Goldstein>When you love mermaids that much-- and dolphins-- you don't keep that to yourself.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>当你那么热爱美人鱼——还有海豚时——你不会把它藏在心里的。

00:13:13.060 --> 00:13:33.000
<v Fred Schultz>My outgoing message, first it had Ariel from The Little Mermaid singing "Part of Your World," singing like, "part of your world. What would I give if I could live out of these waters?" And then I jump on and say, hi, please leave a message for me and The Little Mermaid. And then you hear beep.
<v Fred Schultz>我的来电留言，最开始是《小美人鱼》里的爱丽儿唱着《成为你世界的一部分》，唱着：“成为你世界的一部分。如果我能离开这片水域，我愿意付出什么？”然后我接着说：你好，请给我和小美人鱼留个言。然后你会听到“哔”的一声。

00:13:33.000 --> 00:13:41.400
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Now put yourself in his mother's shoes. To hear Joan Schultz, Fred's mom, tell the story, that outgoing message was like a call to arms.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>现在，设身处地想一下他母亲的处境。听听琼·舒尔茨，也就是弗雷德的妈妈讲这个故事，那条来电留言就像是战斗的号角。

00:13:41.400 --> 00:14:03.940
<v Joan Schultz>I hear The Little Mermaid music. And he said, sorry I can't answer you now. Please leave a message for me and The Little Mermaid. Well, that's all I had to hear. I was so infuriated and so incensed, that without even thinking-- and I never ever say this word-- I said, Fred, you and The Little Mermaid can both go [BLEEP] yourselves. And I slammed the phone down.
<v Joan Schultz>我听到了《小美人鱼》的音乐。然后他说，抱歉我没法接听。请给我和小美人鱼留个言。好吧，听到这儿我就够了。我当时真是气疯了，火冒三丈，还没经过大脑思考——我从来不说那个脏话——我直接说：弗雷德，你和《小美人鱼》都去[消音]吧。然后我狠狠地挂断了电话。

00:14:03.940 --> 00:14:20.910
<v Jonathan Goldstein>So late that night, studying in his dorm room for finals, Fred finally decides to forward the message to his friend Jeff. Then Fred goes to bed. And by the next morning, he wakes up to discover, that just like one of those guys in one of those movies, his life has suddenly become forever changed.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>那天深夜，弗雷德在宿舍里为期末考试复习时，终于决定把这条信息转发给他的朋友杰夫。然后弗雷德就睡了。第二天一早，他醒来时发现，就像电影里的那些家伙一样，他的人生突然间被永远改变了。

00:14:20.910 --> 00:14:33.780
<v Fred Schultz>My message machine was blinking that all 10 messages that it accepts are filled. They were all filled with people-- like chains of 20-- it had already gone around to say each chain had hit 20 or 30 people.
<v Fred Schultz>我的留言机闪烁着，显示它能接收的全部10条信息都满了。全都是人们留的——像20条连锁信息——它们已经在每一条连锁信息中传开了，每条都传给了20或30个人。

00:14:33.780 --> 00:14:37.510
<v Jonathan Goldstein>How many people heard it over the course of the night?
<v Jonathan Goldstein>那天晚上有多少人听到了？

00:14:37.510 --> 00:14:40.020
<v Fred Schultz>Hundreds had already heard it in the middle of the night.
<v Fred Schultz>深夜时分，已经有数百人听过了。

00:14:40.020 --> 00:14:41.830
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Over the course of how many hours?
<v Jonathan Goldstein>是在多少小时之内？

00:14:41.830 --> 00:14:43.260
<v Fred Schultz>Like four hours.
<v Fred Schultz>大概四个小时吧。

00:14:43.260 --> 00:15:06.140
<v Jonathan Goldstein>What was then to follow for the Schultzes was nothing short of campus-wide celebrityhood. Women ran up to Fred and hugged him. Men envied him for his ability to inspire that much raw transparent hostility in his mother. The phone message was so popular that, like a hit TV show, it spawned spin-offs. Other messages circulated with people commenting on the original.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>随后对舒尔茨一家来说，这简直就是全校范围的名人效应。女生跑过来拥抱弗雷德。男人们羡慕他能激起他母亲如此原始而透明的敌意。这条留言太受欢迎了，以至于像一部热门电视连续剧一样，它衍生出了番外篇。人们开始流传其他留言，对原始版本发表评论。

00:15:06.140 --> 00:15:11.590
<v Woman 3>As a history major, I think we've got to put this into a class struggle perspective. His mother represents--
<v Woman 3>作为一个历史系学生，我认为我们得从阶级斗争的角度来看待这件事。他母亲代表了——

00:15:11.590 --> 00:15:18.320
<v Man 5>From a political science standpoint, I would say that both Fred and his mother are products of the political system.
<v Man 5>从政治学的角度来看，我会说弗雷德和他的母亲都是政治体制的产物。

00:15:18.320 --> 00:15:22.590
<v Man 6>I feel that Mrs. Schultz's sexual desire for her son, Fred, is manifest.
<v Man 6>我觉得舒尔茨夫人对自己儿子弗雷德的性渴望是显而易见的。

00:15:22.590 --> 00:15:27.520
<v Jonathan Goldstein>And Josh was even right about the dance remix version of the message.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>而乔什关于该留言的舞曲混音版的说法竟然也是对的。

00:15:27.520 --> 00:15:33.350
<v Joan Schultz>You and The Little Mermaid can go [BLEEP] yourselves. The Little Mermaid can go [BLEEP] yourselves, yourselves, mother, mother.
<v Joan Schultz>你和《小美人鱼》都去[消音]吧。小美人鱼都去[消音]吧，你们自己，你们自己，母亲，母亲。

00:15:33.350 --> 00:15:54.150
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Although no official at Columbia could confirm this next claim, virtually everyone I spoke to who graduated Fred's year remembers this as a point of fact. The popularity of the Little Mermaid yielded message threads that were too long for the new voice mail technology to handle. And so the messaging service for the whole of Columbia crashed.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>虽然哥伦比亚大学没有官方人员能证实接下来的这一说法，但几乎所有我采访过的与弗雷德同一年毕业的人都将其视为事实。《小美人鱼》的流行产生了一些留言串，长到新的语音信箱技术都无法处理。于是，整个哥伦比亚大学的通讯服务崩溃了。

00:15:54.150 --> 00:16:14.440
<v Jonathan Goldstein>It goes further still. Fred's mother's message went on to become the most crowd-pleasing musical number from the year-end Varsity Show, a time-honored all-male production that goes back to Columbia alumni Rodgers and Hammerstein in the early 1900s. The choreographed routine involved a kick line of hairy-legged men in seashell brassieres and mermaid tails.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>不仅如此。弗雷德母亲的信息后来还成了年终“大学秀”（Varsity Show）中最受观众欢迎的音乐节目，这是一场拥有悠久传统的全男性制作表演，可以追溯到20世纪初哥伦比亚校友罗杰斯和哈默斯坦的时代。编排的舞蹈动作包括一排穿着贝壳胸罩和美人鱼尾巴、腿毛浓密的男人的踢踏舞。

00:16:14.440 --> 00:16:19.010
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Steve Nadick, the show's lyricist, dug out the words and favored me with a few select lines.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>该秀的作词人史蒂夫·纳迪克翻出了歌词，向我展示了几行精选内容。

00:16:19.010 --> 00:16:52.330
<v Steve>Oh, here it is. Look at that. But I don't even know if this is the final wording, because I see some handwritten notes on the side. It starts off, "We beautiful creatures inhabit the sea. Fish-women, frolicking frivolously. Although no one said that we'd have to enjoy-a, it still could be worse. We might be in La Jolla." And we sang "in La Jolla" as Handel's "Messiah." "So it's time to decide what you might want to do. We're not going to wait up all night for you. Goodbye."
<v Steve>噢，在这儿。你看这个。但我甚至不知道这是不是最终版本，因为我看到旁边有一些手写笔记。开头是，“我们这些美丽的生物居住在海洋。鱼女们，轻浮地嬉戏。虽然没人说我们必须享受这一切，但情况可能更糟。我们可能在拉霍亚。”我们用韩德尔《弥赛亚》的旋律唱出了“在拉霍亚”。“所以是时候决定你想做什么了。我们不会整晚在这儿等你。再见。”

00:16:52.330 --> 00:17:08.950
<v Jonathan   Goldstein>As even the most casual viewer of VH1's Behind the Music knows, fame like this doesn't come without a price. When Fred's mother came to New York for her son's graduation, she experienced the darker side of super stardom.
<v Jonathan   Goldstein>正如任何一个VH1《音乐内幕》（Behind the Music）节目的普通观众所知，这样的名声是有代价的。当弗雷德的母亲来到纽约参加儿子的毕业典礼时，她体验到了超级明星黑暗的一面。

00:17:08.950 --> 00:17:21.320
<v Joan Schultz>On Broadway, in restaurants, in the shops there they would say things like, that's Fred's mother. That's the Little Mermaid. And I was mortified. Wherever we were, people would point and laugh and snicker.
<v Joan Schultz>在百老汇，在餐馆，在商店里，他们会说：“那是弗雷德的妈妈。那是小美人鱼。”我感到羞愧难当。无论我们走到哪里，人们都会指指点点、嘲笑讥讽。

00:17:21.320 --> 00:17:38.970
<v Fred Schultz>So she just made it her job, at that point, to just walk up to any random group of people, and just start saying, you don't understand. I never use the F word. He provoked me. He provoked me. So she felt that that was her responsibility, to clear her name, to at least let them know she never curses.
<v Fred Schultz>所以从那时起，她的工作就是走到随便哪群陌生人面前，开始说：“你们不懂。我从不说F开头的脏话。是他激怒了我。是他激怒了我。”所以她觉得那是她的责任，要洗清自己的名声，至少要让他们知道她从不骂人。

00:17:38.970 --> 00:17:52.060
<v Joan Schultz>Before my message came along, the funniest message they had sent around was something like other kids' mothers begging them not to forget to use their rubbers in the rainy season.
<v Joan Schultz>在我的信息火起来之前，他们传阅过的最有趣的留言是其他孩子的妈妈叮嘱他们雨季别忘了戴避孕套之类的。

00:17:52.060 --> 00:18:03.750
<v Jonathan  Goldstein>Here's an example of what Joan Schultz is talking about, one of those feel good homesy messages. This is a message from Huey Hockman's grandparents, making sure he was taking care of his cold.
<v Jonathan  Goldstein>这就是琼·舒尔茨所说的例子，那种感觉温馨的家常信息。这是一条休伊·霍克曼的祖父母发给他的信息，确保他在照顾自己的感冒。

00:18:03.750 --> 00:18:07.610
<v Huey's Grandmother>Huey, we heard you have a cold, darling. We called to see how you feel.
<v Huey's Grandmother>休伊，亲爱的，我们听说你感冒了。我们打电话来看看你感觉怎么样。

00:18:07.610 --> 00:18:08.370
<v Huey's Grandfather>Yeah.
<v Huey's Grandfather>是的。

00:18:08.370 --> 00:18:10.120
<v Huey's Grandmother>And to tell you we love you.
<v Huey's Grandmother>并且告诉你我们爱你。

00:18:10.120 --> 00:18:10.780
<v Huey's Grandfather>Yeah.
<v Huey's Grandfather>是的。

00:18:10.780 --> 00:18:13.830
<v Huey's Grandmother>That's all. We hope you're OK, darling.
<v Huey's Grandmother>就这些。我们希望你没事，亲爱的。

00:18:13.830 --> 00:18:15.110
<v Huey's Grandfather>Yeah, me too.
<v Huey's Grandfather>是的，我也是。

00:18:15.110 --> 00:18:16.080
<v Huey's Grandmother>Good night.
<v Huey's Grandmother>晚安。

00:18:16.080 --> 00:18:18.110
<v Huey's Grandfather>Good night.
<v Huey's Grandfather>晚安。

00:18:18.110 --> 00:18:24.430
<v Joan Schultz>Mine was so far and above that that they won't even go back to the old one.
<v Joan Schultz>我的那条留言水平远超那些，以至于他们连旧的都不会回去听了。

00:18:24.430 --> 00:18:28.450
<v Jonathan  Goldstein>Do you take a certain level of pride in that?
<v Jonathan  Goldstein>你对此感到自豪吗？

00:18:28.450 --> 00:18:31.960
<v Joan Schultz>I guess so. In a strange way, yes.
<v Joan Schultz>我想是的。以一种奇怪的方式，是的。

00:18:31.960 --> 00:18:45.400
<v Jonathan  Goldstein>The Little Mermaid message was everything Josh said that it was. And now that I spoke with everyone about its glory, there was really only one more person left to talk to.
<v Jonathan  Goldstein>《小美人鱼》留言确实如乔什所说的一切。现在，我与每个人谈过它的光辉，真的只剩下最后一个人要聊了。

00:18:45.400 --> 00:18:46.720
<v Josh>What do you want?
<v Josh>你想要什么？

00:18:46.720 --> 00:19:01.850
<v Jonathan Goldstein>I made some calls to Columbia. I spoke to some people who went to school the same time that you did. Yes, I did. And diggy do, you were right. It was all true. The message made a great impact.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>我给哥伦比亚大学打了一些电话。我和一些和你同一时期上学的人聊过。是的，我聊了。Diggy do，你说得对。这一切都是真的。那条留言影响很大。

00:19:01.850 --> 00:19:07.730
<v Josh>Wow, thanks John. Listen, what a bastard you are. I gave you gold. Don't you understand?
<v Josh>哇，谢谢约翰。听着，你真是个混蛋。我给了你金子。你还不明白吗？

00:19:07.730 --> 00:19:13.270
<v Jonathan Goldstein>But anyway, you are missing the point that what I'm saying is that I apologize, because you are right.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>但不管怎样，你没明白重点，我想说的是，我道歉，因为你是对的。

00:19:13.270 --> 00:19:17.970
<v Josh>I diggy don't give a rat's ass.
<v Josh>我一点都不在乎。

00:19:17.970 --> 00:19:29.240
<v Jonathan Goldstein>I am going to read to you a piece of the script that I've written that I'm thinking I might actually end this whole story with, because I want to get some of your feedback. OK?
<v Jonathan Goldstein>我要为你读一段我写的剧本片段，我考虑过用这个来结束整个故事，因为我想听听你的反馈。好吗？

00:19:29.240 --> 00:19:29.870
<v Josh>Oh, I'm ready.
<v Josh>噢，我准备好了。

00:19:29.870 --> 00:19:50.300
<v Jonathan  Goldstein>I would say something to the effect of, "and so a recording intended for one person unintentionally became the beloved property of thousands. And in so happening, the message went from being what might have been considered a rather tragic personal artifact that spoke of dysfunction to becoming a triumph of contemporary American humor.
<v Jonathan  Goldstein>我会说类似这样的话：“于是，一段原本只打算给一个人听的录音，无意中成了成千上万人的挚爱珍宝。在这过程中，这段留言从一个可能被视为充满功能障碍的悲剧性个人物品，变成了一个当代美国幽默的胜利之作。”

00:19:50.300 --> 00:19:54.120
<v Josh>What is that? That's public radio wussy talk. Be a man.
<v Josh>那是什么？那是公共广播的娘娘腔谈话。像个男人一样。

00:19:54.120 --> 00:20:00.030
<v Jonathan Goldstein>No, a part of that whole statement is that I'm actually saying to you, you were right and I was wrong.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>不，那整段话的一部分其实是在对你说，你是对的，而我错了。

00:20:00.030 --> 00:20:19.110
<v Josh>All right. Whatever. If you want to talk that fancy talk, you do your thing. But don't drag me into your serious voice nonsense. And you get to speak in this stentorian tone, like, and then America laughed at this inadvertent piece of comedy. I'm John Goldstein.
