00:21:20.250 --> 00:21:25.930
<v Kevin Murphy>Not too long ago, a few of my friends and I were hanging out in my dorm room, watching TV.
<v Kevin Murphy>不久前，我和几个朋友在我的宿舍里闲逛，看着电视。

00:21:25.930 --> 00:21:34.220
<v Ira Glass>Kevin was interested in using this powerful technology to record a message for one man at one business who has been bothering him. Here it is.
<v Ira Glass>凯文想利用这种强大的技术录制一条留言，给一家一直困扰他的企业的老板听。就在这儿。

00:21:34.220 --> 00:22:06.240
<v Kevin  Murphy>This recording is for the Pizza Pipeline in Moscow, Idaho. Not too long ago, a few of my friends and I were hanging out in my dorm room, watching TV. Same old stuff, nothing new. As college students, when hunger calls, we call for pizza. I pick up the phone behind me. I've got the coupon book out. I decide to go for the $10.99 special, drinks and crazy bread included. I dial the number.
<v Kevin  Murphy>这段录音是给爱达荷州莫斯科市的披萨管道（Pizza Pipeline）店的。不久前，我和几个朋友在我的宿舍里闲逛，看着电视。老样子，没什么新鲜的。作为大学生，当饥饿召唤时，我们就会订披萨。我拿起身后的电话。我拿出优惠券手册。我决定选择10.99美元的特价套餐，包含饮料和疯狂面包。我拨通了号码。

00:22:06.240 --> 00:22:50.160
<v Kevin  Murphy>This is when the beast takes over. Anxiety climbs with every ring of the phone. This is what I feel whenever I have to call you up, Mr. Pizza Man. I've dealt with my stutter every second of my life. Is it too much to ask for you to deal with it for a couple of minutes? You interrupt, you cut me off, you speak as if I am this nuisance to your day. Well, today I've saved you this inconvenience. I've edited out all my stutters and pauses to make it easier for you. That is what you want, isn't it?
<v Kevin  Murphy>这就是野兽接管身体的时候。随着电话的每一次铃响，焦虑感都在攀升。每当我不得不打电话给你时，我都会感到这种焦虑，披萨先生。我这辈子每分每秒都在与我的口吃作斗争。让你应付几分钟难道就那么难吗？你打断我，挂断我，说话的态度仿佛我是你一天里的麻烦。好吧，今天我已经省去了你的不便。我编辑掉了我所有的口吃和停顿，好让你更容易听懂。这正是你想要的，不是吗？

00:22:50.160 --> 00:23:31.120
<v Kevin  Murphy>When my anxiety has reached its peak by the third ring, I hear you bellow, hello. I attempt to respond, but nothing comes out. This is the most difficult point in a phone call for stutterers, getting past the point of being a suspected prank caller. Hello, you say again, impatiently. I still can't get a sound out. Please don't hang up on me. Please stay with me. This it finally pops out, my name is Kevin, and I stutter. I would like the $10.99 special with pepperoni.
<v Kevin  Murphy>当我的焦虑在第三声铃响时达到顶点，我听到你咆哮着说：“喂。”我试图回应，但什么声音都没出来。这是口吃者在电话里最艰难的时刻，渡过被误认为是恶作剧电话的阶段。“喂，”你不耐烦地又说了一遍。我还是发不出声音。求你别挂我电话。求你耐心听着。终于蹦出来了，我叫凯文，我有口吃。我想要那份10.99美元的加香肠特价披萨。

00:23:31.120 --> 00:23:56.500
<v Kevin  Murphy>There's no response. I fear I've lost you. Are you there, I ask. My friends act as though paying attention to the TV. You finally respond, yeah. Thank God, I think, I got him, hook, line, and sinker. From here on out, all I have got to do is reel him in.
<v Kevin  Murphy>没有回应。我担心你已经挂了。我问：“你在吗？”我的朋友们表现得好像在专心看电视。你终于回应了，“嗯。”谢天谢地，我想，我钓到他了，从头到尾。从现在起，我要做的就是把他收上来。

00:23:56.500 --> 00:24:24.830
<v Kevin  Murphy>But then you ask what kind of drinks we want. Oh no, I didn't even think of this. Now I've got to tell you to hold on. That has an H sound in it. I hate H's. They are always a killer. They start but do not end. They drain my lungs' air capacity. But I finally get it out and ask everyone what kind of drinks they want. All Cokes. That's easy enough.
<v Kevin  Murphy>但接着你问我们想要什么饮料。噢，不。我根本没想到这茬。现在我得让你等一下。那里面有个“H”的音。我讨厌“H”。它们总是杀手。它们能开始却不能结束。它们耗尽了我肺部的空气容量。但我终于发出了声音，问大家想要什么饮料。全都要可乐。这倒是挺容易的。

00:24:24.830 --> 00:24:46.330
<v Kevin  Murphy>Oh, no. Oh, no. I don't have any cash. This means I am going to have to put it on my bank card. That is one heck of a lot of numbers. I break the bad news to you. And I can hear the oh crap in the silence of your voice.
<v Kevin  Murphy>噢，不。噢，不。我没带现金。这意味着我得刷银行卡。那可是一长串数字。我告诉你这个坏消息。我能从你沉默的声音里听到那种“噢，糟了”的感觉。

00:24:46.330 --> 00:25:06.020
<v Kevin  Murphy>You carry on with this curt annoyed voice. You obviously couldn't be bothered by such a nuisance. To think I would have the nerve to call you and waste so much of your valuable time. If you knew what I go through, Mr. Pizza Man, I'm sure you wouldn't act this way.
<v Kevin  Murphy>你用那种简短烦躁的语气继续说话。你显然懒得理会这种麻烦。真想不到我竟然有胆量打电话浪费你这么多宝贵的时间。如果你知道我经历了什么，披萨先生，我相信你就不会这样了。

00:25:06.020 --> 00:25:26.810
<v Kevin  Murphy>You know what, Ira? I don't think this is working. Turn off that editing equipment. It's not for me. Stuttering is a part of my life. Without it, I'm not myself.
<v Kevin  Murphy>你知道吗，艾拉？我觉得这行不通。关掉那个剪辑设备吧。它不适合我。口吃是我生活的一部分。没有它，我就不是我自己了。

00:25:26.810 --> 00:26:27.960
<v Kevin  Murphy>OK. You see? One thing that makes it tricky about stuttering, Mr. Pizza Man, is when I'm afraid I'm going to stutter, it makes me stutter more. So one way I can actually stutter less is by forcing myself to stutter now and then. It relieves the tension. So if you can relax, Mr. Pizza Man, and let me stutter, I'll take less of your time. But I would appreciate a little more consideration. Sincerely, your loyal customer, Kevin Murphy.
<v Kevin  Murphy>好的。你看见了吗？关于口吃有一点很棘手，披萨先生，那就是当我害怕自己会口吃时，它反而让我更严重。所以实际上我能少口吃的一个方法就是强迫自己时不时地口吃一下。这能缓解紧张感。所以，如果你能放松点，披萨先生，让我口吃，我会少花你点时间。但我希望你能多一点体谅。真诚的，你的忠实客户，凯文·墨菲。

00:26:27.960 --> 00:26:50.040
<v Ira  Glass>Kevin Murphy, from Idaho State University. He asks us to mention-- and we're glad to mention-- the National Stuttering Association. He says contacting them changed his life, led him to learn how to handle his stuttering. Their web address, www.westutter.org.
<v Ira  Glass>来自爱达荷州立大学的凯文·墨菲。他让我们提到——我们也乐于提到——全国口吃协会。他说联系他们改变了他的人生，让他学会了如何处理口吃。他们的网站地址是：www.westutter.org。

00:26:50.040 --> 00:26:57.440
<v Ira  Glass>Coming up, people recording love and people recording war. In a minute, from Chicago Public Radio and Public Radio International when our program continues.
<v Ira  Glass>接下来，人们录制爱情与战争。一分钟后，来自芝加哥公共广播电台和国际公共广播电台的节目继续播出。

00:26:57.440 --> 00:27:25.820
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Most weeks on our program we invite writers and reporters to put together stories on various topics. Today we are trying something completely different. Every story in the show today is built out of tapes that were made for just one person, not for a national radio audience, recordings made for just one person. We have arrived at Act Three of our show. Act Three, War Story.
<v Ira Glass>这是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。我们节目的大多数周，我们都会邀请作家和记者撰写各种主题的故事。今天，我们尝试了一些完全不同的东西。今天节目中的每个故事都是由只为某一个人制作的磁带组成的，而不是为了国家电台的听众，是只为某个人制作的录音。我们已经进入了节目的第三幕。第三幕，战争故事。

00:27:25.820 --> 00:27:54.580
<v Ira Glass>During the first Persian Gulf War, back in 1991, John Brasfield was an Army scout. That meant that he and three other guys would drive out ahead of everyone else in their unit. They were in a Humvee, which is light-skinned, no real armor, one big gun on it to defend themselves, and some radio equipment. And what they would do is they would drive out, they would spot the enemy, they would call in bombs and troops and tanks on the positions that they spotted. And he took along a little cassette recorder to make tapes for his wife.
<v Ira Glass>在1991年的第一次波斯湾战争期间，约翰·布拉斯菲尔德是一名陆军侦察兵。这意味着他和另外三个人会开着车跑在部队所有人的最前面。他们开的是悍马车，那是轻型外壳，没有真正的装甲，上面装有一门大炮用于自卫，还有一些无线电装备。他们要做的是开车出去，发现敌人，然后呼叫轰炸、部队和坦克来攻击他们发现的目标。他随身带了一台小卡带录音机，为妻子录制磁带。

00:27:54.580 --> 00:28:07.200
<v John Brasfield>And I had made the tapes in case I was killed, that my family might know what happened to me in those last few moments, what actually took place. Maybe if I didn't die instantly, I'd be able to say goodbye to them.
<v John Brasfield>我录这些磁带是为了以防万一我战死了，我的家人能知道在最后时刻发生了什么，实际上经历了什么。如果我没能当场毙命，或许我还能和他们告别。

00:28:07.200 --> 00:28:10.480
<v Ira  Glass>And so when would you record?
<v Ira  Glass>那么你什么时候会录音？

00:28:10.480 --> 00:28:22.850
<v John Brasfield>I would record any time I thought a significant event was going to take place. If I felt like we were getting ready to go into a battle or something had already started, I might push the record button, and go ahead and get the tape rolling.
<v John Brasfield>只要我觉得会有什么重大事件发生，我就会录音。如果我觉得我们准备进入战斗或者战斗已经开始，我可能会按下录音键，让磁带开始转动。

00:28:22.850 --> 00:28:34.970
<v Ira Glass>Well let's go through some of these. Let me ask you to explain what, for example, is happening in this one.
<v Ira Glass>我们来看看其中一些。让我请你解释一下，比如这个片段发生了什么。

00:28:34.970 --> 00:28:42.670
<v John  Brasfield>You never want to go through this. You never want to go through this.
<v John  Brasfield>你永远都不会想经历这个。你永远都不想经历这个。

00:28:42.670 --> 00:28:45.930
<v Ira Glass>What is this? Where are we?
<v Ira Glass>这是什么？我们在哪？

00:28:45.930 --> 00:29:07.260
<v John Brasfield>This was, basically, the first artillery attack that I had sat through. We were sitting under a pretty intense artillery barrage. And I was just, basically, telling my wife-- and I had a small son at that time-- that, basically, you never want to go through this type of fighting or conflict. It was pretty scary at the time.
<v John Brasfield>这基本上是我经历的第一次炮火袭击。我们当时正坐在相当猛烈的炮火覆盖下。我基本上是在告诉我的妻子——我当时有个小儿子——你绝对不想经历这种战斗或冲突。那时候真的挺吓人的。

00:29:07.260 --> 00:29:12.220
<v Ira Glass>Now you had trained for this kind of thing. How was actually being in the real thing different from the training?
<v Ira Glass>你训练过应对这类事情。实战和训练有什么不同？

00:29:12.220 --> 00:29:33.900
<v John Brasfield>Well, they say train as you fight. And I guess it's a lot louder. We had done simulations of this, where they have small TNT chargers that they explode around you while you do your training, but nothing like the real thing coming down and just kind of shaking your nerves up.
<v John Brasfield>嗯，他们说像战斗一样训练。我想，实战声音大得多。我们做过那种模拟训练，训练时周围会有小型TNT炸药爆炸，但没有什么能比得上真实的炮弹落下来，把你的神经震得七荤八素。

00:29:33.900 --> 00:29:49.610
<v Ira   Glass>Now you don't really say that much to your wife on most of the tapes. Usually, it's just long stretches of bombs going off. Let me play another one, and ask you to just explain who is talking in this. [GUNFIRE]
<v Ira   Glass>你大部分磁带里没和妻子说多少话。通常只是炮弹爆炸的长长片段。让我播放另一段，请你解释一下这是谁在说话。[枪声]

00:29:49.610 --> 00:29:50.540
<v Frank Carlson>[BLEEP]
<v Frank Carlson>[消音]

00:29:50.540 --> 00:29:53.090
<v John Brasfield>I've got my tape recorder on.
<v John Brasfield>我的录音机开着呢。

00:29:53.090 --> 00:29:55.700
<v Frank Carlson>Really?
<v Frank Carlson>真的吗？

00:29:55.700 --> 00:29:57.360
<v John  Brasfield>Yeah.
<v John  Brasfield>是啊。

00:29:57.360 --> 00:30:00.790
<v Frank Carlson>Damn, I didn't think of that.
<v Frank Carlson>该死，我没想到这茬。

00:30:00.790 --> 00:30:21.710
<v John Brasfield>This is one of my friends, Frank Carlson. And we had just moved on to what's called a screen line. And it's our job to report any enemy that would come through our position. And we were just having a conversation. Basically we've stopped, we're resting, having a conversation about some events that had taken place earlier that day.
<v John Brasfield>这是我的一个朋友，弗兰克·卡尔森。我们刚转移到所谓的屏障线（screen line）。我们的工作是报告任何进入我们阵地的敌人。我们当时只是在聊天。基本上我们停下来休息，正在谈论那天早些时候发生的一些事情。

00:30:21.710 --> 00:30:31.000
<v Frank Carlson>I'll tell you what was awesome, was when we were on the road. We stopped, and we fired that [UNINTELLIGIBLE], I almost ran over two guys. They were laying on the road.
<v Frank Carlson>跟你说，最牛的是我们在路上的时候。我们停下来，射击了那个[听不清]，我差点碾过两个人。他们躺在路上。

00:30:31.000 --> 00:30:32.240
<v John Brasfield>On the road?
<v John Brasfield>在路上？

00:30:32.240 --> 00:30:33.100
<v Frank Carlson>Yeah.
<v Frank Carlson>是啊。

00:30:33.100 --> 00:30:34.170
<v John Brasfield>I didn't see them.
<v John Brasfield>我没看见他们。

00:30:34.170 --> 00:30:39.350
<v Frank Carlson>They were like this. If I hadn't seen them, I would have run over them.
<v Frank Carlson>他们就像这样。如果我没看见他们，我就碾过去了。

00:30:39.350 --> 00:30:43.910
<v Ira Glass>And so in that situation is there just a lot of sitting there and chewing the fat?
<v Ira Glass>在那种情况下，是不是有很多时间坐在那儿侃大山？

00:30:43.910 --> 00:30:58.330
<v John  Brasfield>Basically, at that point in time, there was nothing we could do. We had put ourselves out in the open. And we felt about as secure as you could feel in that type of situation. And we were just going about business as normal, waiting for this to all pass by.
<v John  Brasfield>基本上，在那时候，我们什么也做不了。我们把自己暴露在空旷地带。我们感觉那种情况下能感受到的安全感也就那样了。我们只是照常行事，等待这一切过去。

00:30:58.330 --> 00:31:01.530
<v Ira Glass>And are there tanks between you and the enemy?
<v Ira Glass>你们和敌人之间有坦克吗？

00:31:01.530 --> 00:31:07.820
<v John Brasfield>No. No, we were the lead element. There would have been us between the tanks and the enemy.
<v John Brasfield>没有。没有，我们是先头部队。在我们和敌人之间，只有我们。

00:31:07.820 --> 00:31:14.170
<v Ira Glass>And your weapon, the weapon mounted on your vehicle, could it shoot back at them? Or was it too far away for that particular weapon?
<v Ira Glass>那你们的武器，装在车上的武器，能回击他们吗？还是太远了？

00:31:14.170 --> 00:31:15.440
<v John Brasfield>No, much too far away.
<v John Brasfield>不，太远了。

00:31:15.440 --> 00:31:20.040
<v Ira Glass>And so you're just sitting there without any kind of armor on you, just sitting there waiting.
<v Ira Glass>所以你们就坐在那儿，没有任何装甲保护，就坐在那儿等？

00:31:20.040 --> 00:31:29.760
<v John Brasfield>Sitting out in the middle of the desert, having a conversation, just kind of waiting for the next order to either move forward or call it quits for the night.
<v John Brasfield>坐在沙漠中间，聊着天，等着下一个命令，要么继续前进，要么收工休息。

00:31:29.760 --> 00:31:34.630
<v Ira  Glass>That does not sound like a very pleasant job.
<v Ira  Glass>听起来这工作不太愉快。

00:31:34.630 --> 00:32:10.280
<v John Brasfield>It's really hard to relay your combat experiences of what you went through to a person that has not been in that situation before. When you are actually in the battle, you have this immense fear of being killed. I just felt like-- when I was sitting through those artillery attacks, I just knew at any second one of those artillery rounds was going to land in my lap and kill me. That is just what it felt like. They are exploding all around you. And you just have this immense terror that the next one that goes off, you're not even going to know it's there. And it's just going to blow you to bits. And then you would go in, and you engage these guys that were firing at you, and destroy them. And you'd be elated. They died, you didn't. You're alive. All right.
<v John Brasfield>要把你经历过的战斗体验传达给一个从没经历过的人，真的很难。当你身处战场时，那种对死亡的恐惧是巨大的。我只是觉得——当坐在炮火覆盖下时，我知道任何一秒钟都有炮弹落在我膝盖上把我炸死。感觉就是这样。它们在你周围爆炸。你有一种巨大的恐惧，担心下一枚响的时候，你甚至不知道它在那儿。然后你会瞬间被炸成碎片。接着你要去和那些向你开火的家伙交战，摧毁他们。然后你会感到欣喜若狂。他们死了，你没死。你还活着。好吧。

00:32:10.280 --> 00:32:22.680
<v John Brasfield>And then you move on to the next little conflict. And then you'd be back down in the pits of fear, of you're just going to die on this. And then you get through, and you destroy them, and you're elated again. It was just an emotional roller coaster going through that.
<v John Brasfield>然后你进入下一个小冲突。接着你又会回到恐惧的深渊，感觉自己就要在那场战斗中死掉。然后你挺过去，摧毁了他们，又感到狂喜。这真是情感上的过山车。

00:32:22.680 --> 00:32:41.470
<v John Brasfield>And about the only people I could really-- besides soldiers that have been in the same situation, would probably be a victim of a violent crime, that has gone through that fear of-- they just know that they're going to die, because that person standing on the other side of the gun is just going to pull the trigger. And then when they make it out of that, the elation that they feel, I am alive.
<v John Brasfield>除了经历过同样情况的士兵，我真的能（感同身受）的人可能就是暴力犯罪的受害者，经历过那种死亡的恐惧——他们知道自己要死了，因为站在枪口另一端的那个人就要扣动扳机。然后当他们死里逃生时，那种狂喜，我活着。

00:32:41.470 --> 00:32:46.860
<v Frank  Carlson>I've come to the conclusion that if you can hear the artillery, you are still alive.
<v Frank  Carlson>我得出一个结论：如果你能听到炮火声，那你还活着。

00:32:46.860 --> 00:32:49.290
<v John Brasfield>Is that incoming?
<v John Brasfield>那是炮火逼近了吗？

00:32:49.290 --> 00:32:51.430
<v Frank Carlson>Yeah.
<v Frank Carlson>是啊。

00:32:51.430 --> 00:32:52.810
<v John Brasfield>There?
<v John Brasfield>在那里？

00:32:52.810 --> 00:32:56.230
<v Frank Carlson>Exactly.
<v Frank Carlson>没错。

00:32:56.230 --> 00:33:11.030
<v John Brasfield>The last round you heard, where it kind of shut the tape recorder down, exploded right behind us. They were just kind of feeling around for us, where we were. And they had us pretty much. We had been sitting in the same location for a while.
<v John Brasfield>刚才你听到的最后一轮，录音机录着录着就关了，它就在我们身后爆炸了。他们只是在摸索我们的位置。他们基本已经锁定我们了。我们在同一个位置坐了有一阵子了。

00:33:11.030 --> 00:33:27.990
<v John Brasfield>Come on. Do your [BLEEP] job, artillery. Take out their [BLEEP]. I don't [BLEEP] care if you [BLEEP] kill [UNINTELLIGIBLE] [BLEEP] artillery [UNINTELLIGIBLE]. Just kill them. [BLEEP] kill them.
<v John Brasfield>来啊。干你们的[消音]工作吧，炮兵。把他们的[消音]给干掉。我不在乎你们[消音]杀死[听不清][消音]炮兵[听不清]。干掉他们。[消音]干掉他们。

00:33:27.990 --> 00:33:29.860
<v Soldier>Man, they're firing up some [BLEEP].
<v Soldier>伙计，他们正在发射一些[消音]。

00:33:29.860 --> 00:33:34.190
<v Ira Glass>What is it like for you to hear these tapes?
<v Ira Glass>听这些磁带对你来说是什么感觉？

00:33:34.190 --> 00:33:53.140
<v John  Brasfield>It gives me an adrenaline rush. It kind of brings me back to the battle. Sometimes it's more than I really care to think about, because it brings back a lot of aspects that the mind has kind of forgotten, and has done that for a reason.
<v John  Brasfield>它给了我一阵肾上腺素。它把我带回了战场。有时候，这超出了我想考虑的范围，因为它带回了大脑已经遗忘的很多方面，而遗忘是有原因的。

00:33:53.140 --> 00:34:01.520
<v Ira Glass>I know that that's one reason why you were hesitant to talk to us, that your wife was concerned about you listening back to these tapes and thinking through all this again.
<v Ira Glass>我知道这也是你最初对和我们谈话犹豫的原因，你的妻子担心你听这些磁带会再次思考这一切。

00:34:01.520 --> 00:34:08.580
<v John Brasfield>For a couple years after the Gulf War, I had trouble sleeping. And I had nightmares. And she didn't want that stuff to resurface.
<v John Brasfield>海湾战争后的几年里，我失眠，做噩梦。她不希望那些东西重现。

00:34:08.580 --> 00:34:13.570
<v Ira Glass>Did your wife eventually listen to these tapes that you originally made for her?
<v Ira Glass>你的妻子最终听了你最初为她录的这些磁带吗？

00:34:13.570 --> 00:34:15.700
<v John Brasfield>No. She has not listened to those.
<v John Brasfield>没有。她没听过。

00:34:15.700 --> 00:34:28.010
<v Ira Glass>Could you talk about the impulse that made you want to make the tapes, that you wanted her to have a tape if something would happen to you, a tape of the moment that it happened?
<v Ira Glass>你能谈谈让你录制磁带的冲动吗，你希望如果自己出了什么事，她能有一盘磁带，录着那一刻发生的真实情况？

00:34:28.010 --> 00:34:53.390
<v John Brasfield>I think it's tough. As a soldier, I've dealt with friends dying and death. And I think it's the not knowing of the family members that is the bad thing about it. Even if what had taken place, even though it wasn't pleasant, that answers a lot of questions.
<v John Brasfield>我觉得这很沉重。作为一名士兵，我见证过朋友的死亡。我觉得不让家属知道真相是最糟糕的事。即使发生的事不愉快，它也能回答很多问题。

00:34:53.390 --> 00:35:00.080
<v John Brasfield>[MILITARY RADIO NOISES]
<v John Brasfield>[军用无线电噪音]

00:35:00.080 --> 00:35:07.160
<v Ira Glass>Now on February 27, 1991, you made some recordings. Let me just ask you to explain what's going on in this first one.
<v Ira Glass>现在在1991年2月27日，你录了一些音。让我请你解释一下第一段发生了什么。

00:35:07.160 --> 00:35:27.370
<v John Brasfield>Well, what had happened is, we were on a screen line again. And our tanks and Bradleys had stopped to refuel. This is something that happened about every two to two and a half hours. We happened to be sitting-- part of us happened to be sitting across a major highway that ran between Basra and Baghdad.
<v John Brasfield>嗯，发生的是我们又在屏障线上。我们的坦克和布拉德利战车停下来加油。这大约每两到两个半小时发生一次。我们碰巧坐着——我们一部分人碰巧坐在连接巴士拉和巴格达的一条主要高速公路上。

00:35:27.370 --> 00:35:57.140
<v John Brasfield>And what was happening is the Iraqi forces were retreating out of Basra to Baghdad. And we just happened to be right in the middle of it at that point in time. And these were vehicles. They were buses, trucks, what we would call light-skinned vehicles, or vehicles that we could engage and destroy very easily. So we could actually stop these vehicles on the road. And that is what we started doing. And we got to a point of about 200 plus prisoners that we are taking.
<v John Brasfield>当时发生的是伊拉克军队正在从巴士拉撤退到巴格达。我们正好在那时候卡在中间。那都是车辆。那是公交车、卡车，也就是我们所说的轻型外壳车辆，或者我们可以非常容易交战并摧毁的车辆。所以我们实际上可以在路上拦截这些车。这就是我们开始做的。我们甚至抓了200多名俘虏。

00:35:57.140 --> 00:36:04.320
<v Ira Glass>Now the prisoners are just-- they're just sitting there on the ground, basically, in the middle of where this road is this? Is that the deal?
<v Ira Glass>现在俘虏只是——他们只是坐在那儿，在路中间，是吗？那是怎么回事？

00:36:04.320 --> 00:36:32.180
<v John Brasfield>Exactly. We had stopped them on the road, and had taken them out, and we had-- I guess if you want to call it processed them. We had taken their weapons away from them, and done whatever we needed to do to neutralize them. And they were giving up very easily. They had no will to fight left in them. And when the task force was done-- when they had refueled and re-armed, or whatever they needed to do-- we got the call to go ahead and move out.
<v John Brasfield>没错。我们在路上拦截了他们，把他们带出来，然后我们——如果可以的话称之为处理他们。我们收缴了他们的武器，做了任何需要做的事来把他们中立化。他们投降得很轻松。他们已经没有斗志了。当特遣部队完成任务后——当他们加好油、重新武装好，或者完成了任何需要做的事后——我们接到命令继续前进。

00:36:32.180 --> 00:36:52.510
<v John Brasfield>We had communicated the location of the prisoners back to the battalion command net. The problem with this is we have radios on our vehicles, but not everybody has the radio to the same frequency. So typically what would happen is the battalion commander would have a battalion command frequency, where he would have his commanders up on that frequency.
<v John Brasfield>我们把俘虏的位置传回了营部指挥网。问题在于我们的车上有无线电，但并非每个人都有同一频率的无线电。所以通常的情况是，营长会有一个营指挥频率，而他的连长们都在这个频率上。

00:36:52.510 --> 00:36:55.260
<v Ira Glass>Right. So the commander knows everything that you're saying. He knows.
<v Ira Glass>对。所以指挥官知道你说的一切。他知道。

00:36:55.260 --> 00:37:19.440
<v John Brasfield>That is correct. Basically, we left the prisoners, by themselves, with no weapons, sitting out in the middle of the desert, expecting that the battalion command net would put out that there were prisoners there. Now I don't know if this was ever put out or not. I can't-- but as the infantry moved up and came in the visual range of the prisoners, the infantry began opening fire.
<v John Brasfield>没错。基本上，我们把俘虏留在那儿，他们没有武器，坐在沙漠中间，以为营部指挥网会传出有俘虏在那儿的消息。现在我不知道这是否传达出去了。我无法——但当步兵部队推进并进入俘虏的视野范围时，步兵开始开火。

00:37:19.440 --> 00:37:28.190
<v John Brasfield>Now what you hear on my radio is a call from one of the scouts that was left behind, saying, I hope they know what a Humvee looks like, because he was afraid of being hit by friendly fire.
<v John Brasfield>现在你在我的无线电里听到的是一个留下来的侦察兵的呼叫，说：“我希望他们知道悍马长什么样。”因为他担心被友军误伤。

00:37:28.190 --> 00:37:30.230
<v Ira Glass>He saw the rounds coming down.
<v Ira Glass>他看到了弹药落下来。

00:37:30.230 --> 00:37:33.610
<v John Brasfield>He did. Yes. I never saw the rounds coming down.
<v John Brasfield>他看到了。是的。但我从没看见弹药落下来。

00:37:33.610 --> 00:37:39.670
<v Soldier>The lead company right behind us is buying up all those vehicles there. I hope they understand what a Humvee looks like.
<v Soldier>紧跟在我们后面的连队正在摧毁所有的车辆。我希望他们明白悍马长什么样。

00:37:39.670 --> 00:37:54.380
<v John Brasfield>Vehicles continue to come down the road. And instead of processing prisoners-- the Bradleys have quite a bit of range on them-- they were engaging these vehicles and destroying them. It was not a fair fight.
<v John Brasfield>车辆继续行驶在路上。而不是处理俘虏——布拉德利战车有相当远的射程——他们正在攻击这些车辆并摧毁它们。这不是一场公平的战斗。

00:37:54.380 --> 00:37:58.450
<v Platoon Sergeant>Why, why are we shooting at these people, when they are not shooting at us?
<v Platoon Sergeant>为什么，为什么我们要射杀这些人，当他们没有向我们开火时？

00:37:58.450 --> 00:38:07.910
<v John  Brasfield>I know. They want to surrender. [BLEEP] armored vehicles. And they don't have to blow them apart.
<v John  Brasfield>我知道。他们想要投降。[消音]装甲车辆。他们没必要把它们炸成碎片。

00:38:07.910 --> 00:38:08.870
<v Ira Glass>Now that's you?
<v Ira Glass>那是你吗？

00:38:08.870 --> 00:38:32.460
<v John Brasfield>That was me speaking there. And I was agreeing with the platoon sergeant, Sergeant Mulek, about these guys want to surrender, we've got armor going up against light-skinned vehicles-- trucks, buses, whatever-- that were carrying people back and forth. I didn't feel that there was any need to be killing these people, because they didn't have any fight left in them. All we had to do was take prisoners.
<v John Brasfield>那是我在说话。我同意排长穆莱克中士关于这些人想要投降的观点，我们有装甲车对付轻型外壳车辆——卡车、公交车什么的——正在来来回回载人。我觉得没必要杀这些人，因为他们已经没有斗志了。我们要做的一切就是抓俘虏。

00:38:32.460 --> 00:38:47.910
<v John Brasfield>See those guys toasting the back of that truck? When we drove by? Man. [UNINTELLIGIBLE] They killed a lot of people that didn't have to be killed, because if they would have moved the company up there, everybody would have given up. I guarantee you.
<v John Brasfield>看见那些家伙在那辆卡车后面吗？当我们开过时？伙计。[听不清]他们杀了很多没必要死的人，因为如果他们把连队开过来，每个人都会投降的。我向你保证。

00:38:47.910 --> 00:38:59.600
<v John Brasfield>I was talking about the vehicles that were coming down the road. Vehicles continued to come down the road and I didn't feel that there was any need for the loss of life at that point in time.
<v John Brasfield>我在说那些行驶在路上的车辆。车辆继续行驶在路上，我觉得没必要在那时候夺走生命。

00:38:59.600 --> 00:39:02.900
<v John Brasfield>Why? Why?
<v John Brasfield>为什么？为什么？

00:39:02.900 --> 00:39:04.290
<v Platoon Sergeant>Why do they shoot them?
<v Platoon Sergeant>他们为什么要射杀他们？

00:39:04.290 --> 00:39:05.860
<v Platoon Sergeant>(SUBJECT)JOHN BRASFIELD: They don't have to kill them.
<v Platoon Sergeant>约翰·布拉斯菲尔德：他们没必要杀了他们。

00:39:05.860 --> 00:39:07.480
<v Platoon Sergeant>I know. They don't.
<v Platoon Sergeant>我知道。他们没必要。

00:39:07.480 --> 00:39:12.390
<v John Brasfield>It's a light-skinned vehicle. Now like that one we killed, I understand that. That could kill us.
<v John Brasfield>那是轻型外壳车辆。现在像我们杀的那辆，我明白。那会杀了我们。

00:39:12.390 --> 00:39:14.970
<v Platoon Sergeant>Oh yeah they were [BLEEP] running. They had weapons.
<v Platoon Sergeant>哦，是的，他们[消音]在逃跑。他们有武器。

00:39:14.970 --> 00:39:22.100
<v John Brasfield>They had to do that. That vehicle had to be killed. But these trucks that are full of people. They don't have to kill them.
<v John Brasfield>他们必须那么做。那辆车必须被干掉。但这些满载人的卡车。他们没必要杀了他们。

00:39:22.100 --> 00:39:23.410
<v Platoon  Sergeant>No.
<v Platoon  Sergeant>没有。

00:39:23.410 --> 00:39:33.860
<v Platoon  Sergeant>I felt that, at that point in time, if we would have set up a roadblock, that we could have processed Iraqi prisoners all day. And there was no need for the unnecessary taking of human life.
<v Platoon  Sergeant>在那时候，我觉得如果我们设个路障，我们可以整天处理伊拉克俘虏。根本没必要造成不必要的人员伤亡。

00:39:33.860 --> 00:39:41.810
<v Platoon  Sergeant>What gets me is these guys don't want to fight. You saw the equipment we blew up. They could have wasted us. They were coming down the road.
<v Platoon  Sergeant>让我受不了的是这些家伙不想打仗。你看见我们炸毁的装备了。他们本来可以干掉我们的。他们正开在路上。

00:39:41.810 --> 00:39:46.150
<v Soldier>This is X-ray. We have got a truck with troops on it, moving down [UNINTELLIGIBLE]
<v Soldier>这是X射线。我们有一辆载着部队的卡车，正在移动[听不清]

00:39:46.150 --> 00:39:48.580
<v John Brasfield>Don't shoot it.
<v John Brasfield>别射击它。

00:39:48.580 --> 00:39:51.690
<v Platoon Sergeant>See? He's telling them to engage.
<v Platoon Sergeant>看见没？他在叫他们进攻。

00:39:51.690 --> 00:40:01.770
<v John Brasfield>Why don't you tell them, sir, they they are willing to surrender? Tell him that. Why don't you tell the commander that? It's murder.
<v John Brasfield>为什么你不告诉他们，长官，他们愿意投降？告诉他。为什么你不告诉指挥官？这是谋杀。

00:40:01.770 --> 00:40:12.150
<v Ira   Glass>You're on here saying, it's murder. Tell the commander that they are willing to surrender. Did anyone tell the commanders that these guys were probably willing to surrender?
<v Ira   Glass>你在无线电里说，“这是谋杀。”告诉指挥官他们愿意投降。有没有人告诉指挥官这些人很可能愿意投降？

00:40:12.150 --> 00:40:27.360
<v John Brasfield>I can't recall if we did or not. I'm sure that the platoon leader did make some reference that these guys didn't have any fight left in them. But I couldn't tell you if that was actually said or not. It just felt like we were doing the wrong thing.
<v John Brasfield>我不记得我们是否说了。我相信排长确实提到过这些家伙没有斗志了。但我无法告诉你是否真的说了。感觉就是我们在做错误的事。

00:40:27.360 --> 00:40:42.030
<v Ira    Glass>Now since then, you have had a lot more combat experience. This is still pretty early in your combat career. Just explain briefly where else you served and where else you saw combat.
<v Ira    Glass>从那时起，你有过更多战斗经验。这还是你战斗生涯的早期。简单说明一下你在哪儿服役，在哪儿见过战斗。

00:40:42.030 --> 00:40:55.280
<v John Brasfield>Well, I was in the Gulf War. I finished that out. And I've also spent time in the Bosnia Kosovo conflict. With the Kosovo conflict, flying military intelligence missions for that.
<v John Brasfield>嗯，我参加了海湾战争。我完成了那场战争。我还参加了波斯尼亚和科索沃冲突。在科索沃冲突中，执行军事侦察任务。

00:40:55.280 --> 00:41:03.610
<v Ira Glass>And with more combat experience, do you feel the same way, that it was wrong for the commanders to just fire on these guys and not try to get them to surrender?
<v Ira Glass>有了更多战斗经验，你还觉得指挥官向他们开火而不试图让他们投降是错误的吗？

00:41:03.610 --> 00:41:22.700
<v John Brasfield>No, actually my view has changed quite a bit. It has done almost a 180. And I feel now that what we did was right, that we were not actually murdering people unnecessarily. We were soldiers, they were soldiers. There is risk. They knew what the risk is.
<v John Brasfield>不，实际上我的观点已经改变了很多。几乎180度大转弯。我现在觉得我们所做的是对的，我们实际上并没有无谓地谋杀人。我们是士兵，他们是士兵。有风险。他们知道风险是什么。

00:41:22.700 --> 00:41:42.380
<v John Brasfield>People that haven't been exposed, they may listen to those tapes and still feel like it was murder. As I grew a little bit more battle hardened, I realized there are risks. And they were risks that those soldiers were willing to take. They were risks I was taking at the same time. It could have been me that was killed.
<v John Brasfield>没经历过的人可能会听这些磁带，仍然觉得那是谋杀。随着我变得更久经沙场，我意识到存在风险。那些是那些士兵愿意承担的风险。那也是我当时承担的风险。死的人可能是我的。

00:41:42.380 --> 00:42:01.270
<v Ira  Glass>This particular set of events on February 27, 1991 has gotten a certain amount of press coverage. Seymour Hersh wrote about it in The New Yorker. It was on ABC News. It has gotten a certain amount of coverage. Do you feel like the media's attention to it and the public's attention to it misunderstands it?
<v Ira  Glass>1991年2月27日这具体的事件在媒体上受到了一定的关注。西摩·赫什在《纽约客》上写过这件事。美国广播公司新闻台也报道过。受到了一定的关注。你觉得媒体的注意和公众的注意是否误解了它？

00:42:01.270 --> 00:42:33.980
<v John Brasfield>The information that was put out in ABC News and the Hersh article, I think, was kind of one-sided. They used parts of my tape in the Hersh article. And they took the tape out of context to kind of support what I felt was their point of view. And they were trying to build a case that American soldiers had killed innocent POWs illegally, and that everybody knew what was going on.
<v John Brasfield>ABC新闻和赫什文章中发布的信息，我觉得有点片面。他们在赫什文章中使用了我的磁带片段。他们把磁带断章取义，来支持他们眼中的观点。他们试图建立一个案例，证明美国士兵非法杀害了无辜战俘，而且每个人都知道发生了什么。

00:42:33.980 --> 00:42:58.510
<v John Brasfield>It's more of a deal if you have another My Lai Massacre during the Gulf War than if you actually say that if prisoners were killed, it was because of lack of communication. It was almost like a friendly fire accident. If they were killed, if they indeed were killed, I imagine it was a communication problem that took place, and that the people that killed them-- they wouldn't have done it intentionally-- did not know that these were prisoners, did not know these people were not armed, and did not know they did not pose a threat.
<v John Brasfield>如果海湾战争中发生了另一场“美莱村大屠杀”，比仅仅说如果战俘被杀是因为缺乏沟通，更像是个新闻。那简直就像友军误伤事故。如果他们被杀，如果他们确实被杀了，我想那是沟通问题导致的，而且杀死他们的人——他们不会是故意的——并不知道这些人是战俘，不知道这些人没武装，也不知道这些人不构成威胁。

00:42:58.510 --> 00:43:01.310
<v Ira Glass>The guys who were killing them never got word that these guys weren't armed.
<v Ira Glass>杀死他们的家伙从未收到这些人没武装的消息。

00:43:01.310 --> 00:43:30.790
<v John Brasfield>That's right. It's more of a story when we have American soldiers that are renegades out there, intentionally killing enemy prisoners of war. And that's not the case. If you sit there, and you listen to the tapes, and you listen to what was going on, there was a lot of confusion on the battlefield. And there's no proof-- even the scouts, even though they felt like the prisoners could have been killed, or they saw rounds exploding around them, nobody actually saw anybody killed.
<v John Brasfield>没错。当我们有美国士兵在外当逃兵，蓄意杀害敌方战俘时，这更像是个故事。而事实并非如此。如果你坐下来，听听磁带，听听当时发生了什么，战场上有很多混乱。而且没有证据——即使是侦察兵，尽管他们觉得俘虏可能被杀了，或者他们看到炮弹在他们周围爆炸，没人真正看见任何人被杀。

00:43:30.790 --> 00:43:41.210
<v Ira  Glass>John, do you have friends who you served with in these particular fights who still feel like it was wrong to kill those guys?
<v Ira  Glass>约翰，你有和你一起参加这些战斗的朋友，仍然觉得杀掉那些人是错误的吗？

00:43:41.210 --> 00:44:08.350
<v John Brasfield>Yes, I do. And some of these people have a real difficult time-- they just never-- some of them just have this sense of guilt, where they feel like they have killed people unnecessarily, or were part of a team that killed people unnecessarily, and some of what we did was wrong.
<v John Brasfield>是的，我有。这些人中有些人处境非常艰难——他们只是——有些人有一种负罪感，觉得他们无谓地杀害了人，或者是杀人团队的一员，他们做的一些事是错的。

00:44:08.350 --> 00:44:33.220
<v John Brasfield>I've struggled through that myself. And I feel what we did was right, and what we did was just, and we did what we had to do. And I've moved on with my life. I was able to do that because I came to that conclusion. If I had felt like I had murdered people on the battlefield, I don't think I could live with myself. I think I would have a lot of stress and grief, and not be able to deal with a lot of issues.
<v John Brasfield>我也挣扎过。我感到我们做的是对的，我们所做的是正义的，我们做了必须做的事。我已经走出阴影，过上自己的生活了。我能做到是因为我得出了这个结论。如果我觉得我在战场上杀人，我想我无法与自己和解。我想我会承受巨大的压力和痛苦，无法处理很多问题。

00:44:33.220 --> 00:44:38.700
<v John Brasfield>I think that's basically what it comes down to. You've got to either rationalize what you did, or you're going to live with a lot of guilt.
<v John Brasfield>我想这基本就是结论了。你必须要么把你所做的事合理化，要么就背负着巨大的负罪感活着。

00:44:38.700 --> 00:44:43.520
<v Ira Glass>When you take a moment and you reflect on it, do you feel like there's a part of you that's rationalizing?
<v Ira Glass>当你回顾往事时，你觉得你身上有一部分在合理化吗？

00:44:43.520 --> 00:45:01.600
<v John Brasfield>I did my rationalization some time ago. And I've made my decision on what I did was right. And I stick with it. And I've rationalized at that point. I think you've got to do that so you can live with yourself.
<v John Brasfield>我在一段时间前做过合理化。对于我所做的事是对的，我已经做出了决定。并且我坚持它。我那时确实合理化了。我想你必须这么做，才能过下去。

00:45:01.600 --> 00:45:12.170
<v Ira Glass>John Brasfield now has a job as a flight instructor for civilians in Wichita, Kansas. He is also in the Army Reserve.
<v Ira Glass>约翰·布拉斯菲尔德现在在堪萨斯州威奇托市担任平民飞行教练。他也是陆军预备役成员。

00:45:12.170 --> 00:45:32.430
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - "THE BATTLE IS OVER (BUT THE WAR GOES ON)," BROWNIE MCGHEE AND SONNY TERRY]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - "THE BATTLE IS OVER (BUT THE WAR GOES ON)," BROWNIE MCGHEE AND SONNY TERRY]

00:45:32.430 --> 00:46:02.220
<v Ira Glass>Act Four, Love Story. Well, let's return to the guy who's recording we heard at the beginning of today's program, the percussionist who met the woman in Italy and had started sending her tapes. His name is David Cossin. He sent her over a dozen tapes after their week together, trying to convince her to please, please visit him in New York City.
<v Ira Glass>第四幕，爱情故事。好吧，让我们回到节目开头听到的那个录音的主人身上，那个在意大利遇到这个女人并开始给她寄磁带的打击乐手。他叫大卫·科辛。在他们在一起的那周后，他给她寄了十几盘磁带，试图说服她，求求你，一定要来纽约见他。

00:46:02.220 --> 00:46:12.920
<v Ira Glass>These tapes are such a complete portrait of a certain kind of quest and a certain feeling you get early in a relationship. And he agreed to let us play some excerpts here on the radio.
<v Ira Glass>这些磁带完整地描绘了某种追求和在一段感情早期产生的特定感觉。他同意让我们在电台播出其中的一些片段。

00:46:12.920 --> 00:46:41.160
<v David  Cossin>It's David. I thought I would try to make you a little tape, give you a little tour, or a picture, of how it is in New York. And I guess I will tune in and out with the events of my day. I'm in my apartment. There are instruments everywhere here. [PLAYS INSTRUMENTS]
<v David  Cossin>我是大卫。我想试着给你录一盘小磁带，给你一点纽约的导览，或者画面。我想我会随手记录我生活中的点滴。我在我的公寓里。这里到处都是乐器。[演奏乐器]

00:46:41.160 --> 00:47:08.170
<v David  Cossin>And there's a vibraphone. [PLAYS VIBRAPHONE] Hopefully one day you could see all of this. So I will turn off now, and come back later, and talk to you some more.
<v David  Cossin>还有一个颤音琴。[演奏颤音琴]希望有一天你能亲眼见到所有这些。所以我现在要关掉了，稍后再回来跟你多说几句。

00:47:08.170 --> 00:47:55.140
<v David  Cossin>I'm about to leave my apartment. So you'll hear Chinatown. Usually there are people hanging out in front of my building, old Chinese ladies. So this is my neighborhood. It's nice. There is a bus here, filled with Chinese people going on a tour. And this neighborhood is pretty residential. At nighttime it's very quiet. In the daytime there are a lot of children. You can hear them from my apartment. At lunchtime they're out in the schoolyard playing.
<v David  Cossin>我正要离开我的公寓。所以你会听到唐人街的声音。通常我楼下有很多人闲逛，有中国老太太。所以这就是我的社区。挺好的。这儿有辆巴士，装满了去旅游的中国人。这个社区主要是住宅区。晚上非常安静。白天有很多孩子。你在我的公寓里就能听到。午饭时间他们都在操场上玩。

00:47:55.140 --> 00:48:23.330
<v David  Cossin>And everything that I see, I think of you. So I thought I could just talk to you as I see them. You have to let me know if it is incredibly boring or if you like this. So I'm going to a rehearsal today in Queens, close to my parents' house.
<v David  Cossin>我看到的一切，我都想到了你。所以我想我看到什么就可以直接和你说。如果听起来极其无聊或者你喜欢，你都得告诉我。所以我今天要去皇后区排练，离我父母家很近。

00:48:23.330 --> 00:48:50.790
<v David  Cossin>Hey. I'm in my rehearsal. And I'm sneaking, talking to you. [INSTRUMENTS TUNING UP]
<v David  Cossin>嘿。我正在排练。我偷偷在跟你说话。[乐器调音声]

00:48:50.790 --> 00:49:58.750
<v David  Cossin>Oh, Allesandra. I just read your letter. Wow. I finished reading the letter, and I realized I wasn't even breathing while I was reading. I was holding my breath. And I finished. And I realized I am out of breath, like I just ran up a flight of stairs or something. I miss you so much right now. Oh, please write me more. It's very beautiful, very tender, and making me so happy. And you're great, you're great, you're great. I miss you. I love you. I really have fallen for you. Thank you so much. That was my first letter from you. And to know that-- even though it was a week ago, my feeling is stronger and stronger. So thank you again. That was so nice. I feel so happy right now. I'll speak to you soon.
<v David  Cossin>噢，阿莱桑德拉。我刚读了你的信。哇。我读完信，意识到我在读的时候甚至都没呼吸。我屏住了呼吸。读完了，我意识到我气喘吁吁，就像刚跑上楼梯一样。我现在好想你。噢，请多给我写点。这非常美丽，非常温柔，让我感到好幸福。你真棒，你真棒，你真棒。我想你。我爱你。我真的坠入爱河了。太感谢你了。这是我从你那收到的第一封信。而且知道——尽管那是那一周的事，我的感觉却越来越强烈。再次感谢你。真的太好了。我现在感觉太幸福了。我稍后再和你联系。

00:49:58.750 --> 00:50:39.050
<v David  Cossin>Well, I just talked on the phone a little while ago, Allesandra. And we'll be OK. It's just a feeling. It's not my thought. But just-- I feel confident that everything will be fine, and we will see each other soon. I'm thinking about you.
<v David  Cossin>嗯，我刚通完电话，阿莱桑德拉。我们会没事的。这只是一种感觉。不是我的想法。但只是——我感到自信，一切都会好起来，我们很快就会见面。我一直在想你。

00:50:39.050 --> 00:51:02.610
<v David  Cossin>I couldn't just end like that. I'm back, 10 minutes later than before. Wow, it's even hard to say goodbye to you on a tape. It's hard to say goodbye to you on the phone.
<v David  Cossin>我不能就这样结束。我回来了，比刚才晚了10分钟。哇，要在磁带上跟你说再见都很难。在电话里跟你说再见也很难。

00:51:02.610 --> 00:51:27.100
<v David  Cossin>I went back outside. So maybe I'll just record the sounds of outside for a moment. [SOUNDS OF CHILDREN PLAYING] This is New York, where, hopefully, you will be soon.
<v David  Cossin>我又跑出来了。所以也许我只是录一下外面的声音给听听。[孩子玩耍声] 这是纽约，希望你很快就能来。

00:51:27.100 --> 00:51:44.070
<v Ira   Glass>So David Cossin, who made these tapes, now joins us to explain, first, if they worked.
<v Ira   Glass>那么制作了这些磁带的大卫·科辛现在加入我们，首先，来解释一下它们是否成功了。

00:51:44.070 --> 00:51:52.270
<v David Cossin>Yeah, I mean that was three years ago. And I guess they worked, because I'm sitting in Italy right now with Allesandra.
<v David Cossin>是的，那已经是三年前的事了。我想它们奏效了，因为我现在就坐在意大利，和阿莱桑德拉在一起。

00:51:52.270 --> 00:51:57.860
<v Ira Glass>And how worried were you about how she would react to the first tape?
<v Ira Glass>你当时有多担心她会对第一盘磁带有什么反应？

00:51:57.860 --> 00:52:22.930
<v David Cossin>Yeah, I was a little worried, because it's a fine line between somebody who is intensely trying to connect with somebody with distance and being a stalker or something. I have never really went after something like that before. Allesandra is the first and only person that I ever felt a strong need to keep being connected to on that level, especially for such a quick meeting.
<v David Cossin>是的，我有点担心，因为一个人为了连接远方的人而疯狂努力和成为骚扰者之间只有一线之隔。我从未真正那样追求过什么。阿莱桑德拉是我唯一感到如此强烈需要在那一层面保持连接的人，尤其是对于如此短暂的邂逅而言。

00:52:22.930 --> 00:52:25.750
<v Ira Glass>And Allesandra is sitting right there with you, right?
<v Ira Glass>阿莱桑德拉就坐在你旁边，对吧？

00:52:25.750 --> 00:52:26.830
<v David Cossin>Yes.
<v David Cossin>是的。

00:52:26.830 --> 00:52:28.180
<v Ira Glass>Could you put her on?
<v Ira Glass>你能让她接听吗？

00:52:28.180 --> 00:52:30.440
<v David Cossin>Sure. Hold on one second.
<v David Cossin>没问题。稍等一下。

00:52:30.440 --> 00:52:31.720
<v Allesandra Pomarico>Hello.
<v Allesandra Pomarico>喂。

00:52:31.720 --> 00:52:32.760
<v Ira Glass>Hi, Allesandra.
<v Ira Glass>你好，阿莱桑德拉。

00:52:32.760 --> 00:52:33.750
<v Allesandra Pomarico>Hi.
<v Allesandra Pomarico>你好。

00:52:33.750 --> 00:52:39.090
<v Ira Glass>So could I ask you to talk about what it was like getting the first of these tapes?
<v Ira Glass>那我可以请你谈谈收到这些磁带的第一盘时是什么感觉吗？

00:52:39.090 --> 00:52:43.870
<v Allesandra Pomarico>It was tender. It was really feeling again the presence of somebody.
<v Allesandra Pomarico>很温柔。那真的再次感受到了一个人的存在。

00:52:43.870 --> 00:52:52.100
<v Ira Glass>Before you got the tapes, did it seem like such a serious thing that happened between you and him?
<v Ira Glass>在收到磁带之前，你和他的事看起来有那么严重吗？

00:52:52.100 --> 00:53:58.960
<v Allesandra Pomarico>I have to say, it was serious because it was intense. We met, and we had a wonderful, incredible week. Something really special happened. But then, maybe I started-- more than him, I started to be more rationalistic. I started to step again on earth. So all this was like a romance, but I was rationally thinking, oh, I'm in Rome, he's in New York. And those tapes helped to continue the romance. And I think this was when I actually consciously felt in love with him. I became aware of that. And this helped me to believe in that love. It was easy in that situation to be scared, and to try to hide from your own feelings or your own state of mind.
<v Allesandra Pomarico>我得说，它是严肃的，因为它很强烈。我们相遇，度过了美妙、难以置信的一周。发生了真正特别的事情。但后来，也许我开始——比他更多地——变得理智了。我开始再次脚踏实地。所以这一切就像是一场浪漫，但我理智地想：噢，我在罗马，他在纽约。那些磁带帮助延续了这段浪漫。我想就是在那时，我真正清醒地感觉到爱上了他。我意识到了这一点。这帮助我相信那份爱。在那种情况下，害怕并试图躲避自己的情感或心理状态是很正常的。

00:53:58.960 --> 00:54:08.310
<v Ira Glass>On the tapes, he worries about being too intense or being too boring. Were there moments of the tapes that struck you that way?
<v Ira Glass>在磁带里，他担心自己表现得太强烈或者太无聊。有哪一刻让你有这种感觉吗？

00:54:08.310 --> 00:54:31.530
<v Allesandra Pomarico>No, no, I was never bored. And I never felt this was too much or too intense. It was tender. I was always sorry when the tape finished. I always wanted more. And I rewinded some parts. And I listened to some of these many times.
<v Allesandra Pomarico>没有，没有，我从来没觉得无聊。我也从没觉得那太过分或太强烈。它是温柔的。磁带结束时我总是感到遗憾。我总是想要更多。我回放了一些片段。我听了很多遍。

00:54:31.530 --> 00:54:37.710
<v Ira Glass>He's so in love with you on these tapes.
<v Ira Glass>他在磁带里是那么爱你。

00:54:37.710 --> 00:55:11.600
<v Allesandra Pomarico>I guess he was. And that's why it was so convincing, in a sense. I felt it was true, and it was irresistible. How can I say? I could not resist. It was natural. Really, you have the sensation your destiny was changed. I guess every love story starts in the same way, in a sense.
<v Allesandra Pomarico>我想他是的。这就是为什么它在某种意义上那么有说服力。我感到那是真实的，且令人无法抗拒。怎么说呢？我无法抗拒。那是自然的。真的，你有种宿命被改变的感觉。我想每一个爱情故事在某种意义上都以同样的方式开始。

00:55:11.600 --> 00:55:36.120
<v Ira Glass>Allesandra and David are now married. In the years since we first broadcast this story, they have had a daughter, named Nina, who just turned four.
<v Ira Glass>阿莱桑德拉和大卫现在结婚了。在我们最初播出这个故事后的几年里，他们有了个女儿，叫妮娜，刚刚四岁。

00:55:36.120 --> 00:55:50.120
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - "SEND ME SOME LOVIN," OTIS REDDING]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - "SEND ME SOME LOVIN," OTIS REDDING]

00:55:50.120 --> 00:56:06.050
